<!--
  Author: Kris Hollingsworth
  Class: Math122-Calculus 2
  Date: 7/23/26
  File: E02-C24-05-methods-for-trig-four.oml
  Description:
    Finding useful identities for other pairs.
  Raw Link: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/DoctorHaitch/doenet-math-problems/main/M122-Calculus-2/E02-C24-05-methods-for-trig-four.oml
-->
<cascade>
  <div>
    <p>Recall that <me>\sin^2(x)+\cos^2(x)=1.</me></p>
    <p>
      Similarly,
      <mathInput name="pyth_1" hide="$pyth_1_ans.creditAchieved=1">
        <label><m>1+</m></label>
      </mathInput>
      <mathInput name="pyth_2" hide="$pyth_1_ans.creditAchieved=1">
        <label><m>=</m></label>
      </mathInput>
      <me hide="$pyth_1_ans.creditAchieved=0">1+\cot^2 x = \csc^2 x~ ✅</me>
      <answer name="pyth_1_ans" hide="$pyth_1_ans.creditAchieved=1">
        <award>
          <when>$pyth_1=<m>\cot^2 x</m> and $pyth_2=<m>\csc^2 x</m></when>
        </award>
      </answer>
    </p>
    <p>
      and
      <mathInput name="pyth_3" hide="$pyth_2_ans.creditAchieved=1">
        <label />
      </mathInput>
      <mathInput name="pyth_4" hide="$pyth_2_ans.creditAchieved=1">
        <label><m>+1 =</m></label>
      </mathInput>
      <me hide="$pyth_2_ans.creditAchieved=0">\tan^2 x+1 = \sec^2 x.~ ✅</me>
      <answer name="pyth_2_ans" hide="$pyth_2_ans.creditAchieved=1">
        <award>
          <when>$pyth_3=<m>\tan^2 x</m> and $pyth_4=<m>\sec^2 x</m></when>
        </award>
      </answer>
    </p>
  </div>
  <div>
    <p>Additionally, we have the relationships that</p>
    <problems>
      <title />
      <problem>
        <answer>
          <mathInput><label><m>\frac{d}{dx}\sec(x)=</m></label></mathInput>
          <award><m>\sec(x)\tan(x)</m></award>
        </answer>
      </problem>
      <problem>
        <answer>
          <mathInput><label><m>\frac{d}{dx}\tan(x)=</m></label></mathInput>
          <award><m>\sec^2(x)</m></award>
        </answer>
      </problem>
      <problem>
        <answer>
          <mathInput><label><m>\frac{d}{dx}\cot(x)=</m></label></mathInput>
          <award><m>-\csc^2(x)</m></award>
        </answer>
      </problem>
      <problem>
        <answer>
          <mathInput><label><m>\frac{d}{dx}\csc(x)=</m></label></mathInput>
          <award><m>-\csc(x)\cot(x)</m></award>
        </answer>
      </problem>
    </problems>
  </div>

  <div>
    <p>
      Observe then that the pairs
      <choiceInput inline shuffleOrder name="pair_1">
        <choice name="correct_one_a"><m>\cot(x)</m> and <m>\csc(x)</m></choice>
        <choice><m>\cot(x)</m> and <m>\sec(x)</m></choice>
        <choice><m>\cot(x)</m> and <m>\tan(x)</m></choice>
        <choice><m>\csc(x)</m> and <m>\sec(x)</m></choice>
        <choice><m>\csc(x)</m> and <m>\tan(x)</m></choice>
        <choice name="correct_one_b"><m>\sec(x)</m> and <m>\tan(x)</m></choice>
      </choiceInput> and <choiceInput inline shuffleOrder name="pair_2">
        <choice name="correct_two_a"><m>\cot(x)</m> and <m>\csc(x)</m></choice>
        <choice><m>\cot(x)</m> and <m>\sec(x)</m></choice>
        <choice><m>\cot(x)</m> and <m>\tan(x)</m></choice>
        <choice><m>\csc(x)</m> and <m>\sec(x)</m></choice>
        <choice><m>\csc(x)</m> and <m>\tan(x)</m></choice>
        <choice name="correct_two_b"><m>\sec(x)</m> and <m>\tan(x)</m></choice>
      </choiceInput> are related in similar ways to how <m>\sin(x)</m> and
      <m>\cos(x)</m> are related.
    </p>
    <p>
      <answer>
        <award>
          <when>
            $correct_one_a.selected and $correct_two_b.selected
          </when>
        </award>
        <award>
          <when>
            $correct_one_b.selected and $correct_two_a.selected
          </when>
        </award>
      </answer>
    </p>
  </div>
  <div>
    <p>
      Given this, we can derive similar identities
      for simplifying the process of integration. In this class, we will only
      focus on one remaining set, the other follows almost identically.
    </p>
  </div>
</cascade>